<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Second conditional: <em>step into an imagined situation</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">A past form can create distance without moving the meaning into past time. In <span class="ex">If we had more room, we’d add a studio</span>, the speaker imagines a different situation now. The grammar presents a viewpoint; it does not calculate the real-world probability for us.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">imagined present · remote future · advice</span>
    <span class="tag">if + past form · would / could / might + base verb</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare an open plan with a deliberately distant scenario.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Use past grammar for distance, not past time.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Contrast</b><span>Choose First or Second Conditional from the speaker’s stance.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Explore fictional options, advice, and consequences.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can build positive, negative, and question patterns; use a past form for imagined present or future distance; distinguish an open First Conditional from a remote Second Conditional without claiming objective probability; choose <span class="ex">would, could</span>, or <span class="ex">might</span> for the intended result; use careful <span class="ex">were</span> and natural informal <span class="ex">was</span> accurately; give tactful advice; hear reduced U.S. forms; and lead a clear hypothetical discussion.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved an open First Conditional result, the Past Simple form had, and advice with should.">
    <h3>Three foundations from A2</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the stated future status, verb family, and advice purpose.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Second conditional foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will rent">will rent</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had">had</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="should ask">should ask</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would rented">would rented</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="asked yesterday">asked yesterday</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The grant decision on Friday is open, and the team has a real response plan: If the grant arrives, we <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will rent" data-hint="Use will plus a base verb for the planned result of this open future condition." data-fix="If the grant arrives on Friday, we will rent Hall A.">choose</button> Hall A.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The Past Simple form of <span class="ex">have</span> for every subject is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had" data-hint="Retrieve the second form in have, had, had." data-fix="The Past Simple form of have is had.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A colleague wants direct advice about a smaller pilot: You <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="should ask" data-hint="Should plus a base verb gives direct advice." data-fix="You should ask whether a smaller pilot is possible.">choose</button> whether a smaller pilot is possible.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice an open plan, an imagined world, and advice</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Riverside community studio meeting</h3>
    <p><b>Open decision:</b> <span class="ex">If the grant arrives on Friday, we’ll rent Hall A.</span></p>
    <p><b>Different situation now:</b> <span class="ex">If we had twice the budget, we’d build a recording booth. We do not have that money now.</span></p>
    <p><b>Advice through imagination:</b> <span class="ex">If I were you, I’d start with a smaller pilot.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which condition belongs to a real current plan?</li>
      <li>Which past form describes an imagined present, not a finished past event?</li>
      <li>Which speaker briefly imagines being in another person’s position?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the open grant plan, imagined present budget, past-form distance, hypothetical result, and advice frame.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify the speaker’s viewpoint</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Second conditional discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the grant arrives on Friday">the grant arrives on Friday</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="twice the budget">twice the budget</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had">had</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would build">would build</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="If I were you">If I were you</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The condition connected to the team’s real Friday plan is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the grant arrives on Friday" data-hint="Choose the event the team is genuinely waiting for and has planned around." data-fix="The grant arrives on Friday is the open condition in the First Conditional plan.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The situation the team explicitly says is not true now is having <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="twice the budget" data-hint="The next sentence states that the team does not have this money now." data-fix="Twice the budget is the imagined present situation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The past form that creates distance from the team’s current budget is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had" data-hint="Find the verb after If we in the imagined sentence." data-fix="Had is a past form, but it creates present hypothetical distance here.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The result inside that imagined world is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would build" data-hint="Choose would plus the base verb that describes the hypothetical result." data-fix="Would build is the imagined result of having twice the budget.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The phrase that imagines the listener’s position in order to give advice is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="If I were you" data-hint="Choose the conventional advice frame in which the speaker imagines being the listener." data-fix="If I were you creates an imagined position before the advice result.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: past form as distance, not past time</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The Second Conditional creates a mental space that is separate from the speaker’s current reality or expected path. The condition uses a past form, but the meaning usually concerns now or the future:</p>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <p><span class="ex">current reality: we have one room</span> → <span class="ex">imagined alternative now: if we had two rooms</span> → <span class="ex">result in that world: we would add a studio</span></p>
  </div>
  <p>This distance can serve several purposes: contradict the present facts, explore an unlikely or remote future, dream freely, offer advice, or make a proposal less direct. The grammar reports the speaker’s stance. Two speakers can frame the same possible event differently without either one knowing its objective probability.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>job</th><th>form</th><th>complete example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>condition first</td><td><span class="ex">If + subject + past form, subject + would + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">If we had more room, we would add a studio.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>result first</td><td><span class="ex">Subject + would + base verb + if + subject + past form</span></td><td><span class="ex">We would add a studio if we had more room.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative condition</td><td><span class="ex">if + subject + didn’t + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">If rent didn’t cost so much, we would stay.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative result</td><td><span class="ex">subject + wouldn’t + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">We wouldn’t move if this room were larger.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question</td><td><span class="ex">question word + would + subject + base verb + if...?</span></td><td><span class="ex">What would you change if you led the project?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Use a comma when the if-clause comes first. Do not normally put <span class="ex">would</span> in the condition of a neutral hypothetical: <span class="ex">If we had more room, we would expand</span>. English can use <span class="ex">if you would...</span> for willingness or a formal request, as in <span class="ex">If you would wait here, I’ll check</span>, but that is a different meaning, not the ordinary Second Conditional pattern.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You built a positive condition, result-first order, negative condition, negative result, information question, and modal-distance pattern.">
    <h3>Form check: build both clauses</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Second conditional form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had">had</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would add">would add</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="didn’t cost">didn’t cost</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wouldn’t move">wouldn’t move</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What would you change">What would you change</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="if you led">if you led</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The team has one room now. Imagine the alternative: If we <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had" data-hint="Use the Past Simple form had to create distance from the team’s current one-room reality." data-fix="If we had two rooms, we would create a recording studio.">choose</button> two rooms, we would create a recording studio.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Put the imagined result first: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would add" data-hint="Begin the result with would plus the base verb add." data-fix="We would add evening classes if more tutors were available.">choose</button> evening classes if more tutors were available.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Rent is expensive now. Imagine a lower-cost situation: If rent <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="didn’t cost" data-hint="A negative past-form condition uses didn’t plus the base verb cost." data-fix="If rent didn’t cost so much, the center would stay in this building.">choose</button> so much, the center would stay in this building.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The room is too small, so imagine the opposite: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wouldn’t move" data-hint="Put not on would and keep the base verb move." data-fix="We wouldn’t move if this room were larger.">choose</button> if this room were larger.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Ask for one hypothetical change: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What would you change" data-hint="Begin with What, then would, the subject you, and the base verb change." data-fix="What would you change if you led the project?">choose</button> if you led the project?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Complete the condition with statement order, not question order: What would you change <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="if you led" data-hint="Inside the if-clause, keep the subject you before the past form led." data-fix="What would you change if you led the project?">choose</button> the project?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Five reasons a speaker chooses distance</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>speaker’s purpose</th><th>stated context</th><th>Second Conditional</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>different present</td><td>The center has one room now.</td><td><span class="ex">If it had two rooms, it would offer more classes.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>remote future</td><td>The speaker sees a full renovation as doubtful.</td><td><span class="ex">If the center expanded next year, it would need more staff.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>free imagination</td><td>No decision or probability estimate is needed.</td><td><span class="ex">If the building could talk, what would it say?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>advice</td><td>The speaker imagines the listener’s position.</td><td><span class="ex">If I were you, I’d test the idea first.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>tactful proposal</td><td>The speaker wants a less direct discussion.</td><td><span class="ex">Would it help if we started smaller?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched a different present, remote future, free imagination, advice, and tactful proposal to the context that motivates distance.">
    <h3>Meaning check: identify why the speaker steps back</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Second conditional purpose choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="different present">different present</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="remote future">remote future</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="free imagination">free imagination</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="advice">advice</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="tactful proposal">tactful proposal</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The center has only one room now: <span class="ex">If it had two rooms, it would offer more classes</span> imagines a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="different present" data-hint="The stated fact now is one room; the condition imagines two." data-fix="The sentence imagines a different present in which the center has two rooms.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The director says a full renovation next year is doubtful: <span class="ex">If the center expanded, it would need more staff</span> frames a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="remote future" data-hint="Use the director’s stated doubtful view of the next-year renovation." data-fix="The director frames the possible next-year expansion as a remote future.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>No probability judgment is needed in <span class="ex">If the building could talk, what would it say?</span> The purpose is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="free imagination" data-hint="The question invites a creative answer, not a forecast." data-fix="The talking-building question creates free imagination.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">If I were you, I’d test the idea first</span> imagines the listener’s position to offer <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="advice" data-hint="The result tells the listener what the speaker recommends." data-fix="If I were you introduces advice through an imagined position.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">Would it help if we started smaller?</span> opens a less direct discussion, so its purpose is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="tactful proposal" data-hint="The question softens a possible recommendation instead of commanding the team." data-fix="Would it help if we started smaller? is a tactful proposal.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>First or Second Conditional? Read the speaker’s stance</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>First Conditional presents an open future path and its response: <span class="ex">If the grant arrives Friday, we’ll rent Hall A.</span> Second Conditional deliberately steps back: <span class="ex">If a much larger grant arrived someday, we’d build a studio.</span></p>
  <p>The difference is not a universal percentage. One speaker may say <span class="ex">If I get the role, I’ll move</span> because the interview is active. Another may say <span class="ex">If I got the role, I’d move</span> because they want to explore the idea from a distance. Grammar reveals the chosen framing, not a verified fact about the future.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>viewpoint</th><th>condition</th><th>result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>open future path</td><td><span class="ex">if + present form</span></td><td><span class="ex">will / can / might / imperative + base</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>imagined or remote path</td><td><span class="ex">if + past form</span></td><td><span class="ex">would / could / might + base</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You chose an open plan or a deliberately remote frame from the speaker’s stated stance, not from a mechanical probability rule.">
    <h3>Contrast check: preserve the stated framing</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="First and second conditional contrast choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="arrives, we’ll rent">arrives, we’ll rent</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="arrived, we’d build">arrived, we’d build</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="get, I’ll move">get, I’ll move</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="got, I’d move">got, I’d move</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the speaker’s framing">the speaker’s framing</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The grant result is due Friday, and renting Hall A is the team’s active plan: If the grant <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="arrives, we’ll rent" data-hint="Use the open First Conditional frame stated in the current plan." data-fix="If the grant arrives on Friday, we’ll rent Hall A.">choose</button> Hall A.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The director calls a much larger grant a distant dream: If that grant <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="arrived, we’d build" data-hint="Use a past form and would because the director deliberately frames this as remote." data-fix="If a much larger grant arrived someday, we’d build a studio.">choose</button> a studio.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The interview is active, and the speaker presents moving as a real response plan: If I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="get, I’ll move" data-hint="Use the open frame because the speaker explicitly treats the move as a current plan." data-fix="If I get the role, I’ll move closer to the office.">choose</button> closer to the office.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The speaker is only exploring a distant possibility: If I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="got, I’d move" data-hint="Use the remote frame because the speaker explicitly steps back from the possibility." data-fix="If I got the role, I’d move closer to the office.">choose</button> closer to the office.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The contrast between the last two accurate sentences mainly communicates <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the speaker’s framing" data-hint="The grammar shows how the speaker presents the possibility, not an objectively measured percentage." data-fix="The forms communicate the speaker’s framing of the same possible event.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2><span class="ex">Were</span>, <span class="ex">was</span>, and the advice frame</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In careful hypothetical English, <span class="ex">were</span> can appear with every subject: <span class="ex">If I were, if she were, if the room were</span>. This form is especially common in formal writing and in the conventional advice phrase <span class="ex">If I were you</span>.</p>
  <p>In natural informal U.S. conversation, many speakers also use <span class="ex">was</span> with <span class="ex">I, he, she</span>, and <span class="ex">it</span>: <span class="ex">If I was more organized, I’d volunteer</span>. Do not label that widespread informal pattern as meaningless or impossible. For careful writing and the fixed advice frame, <span class="ex">were</span> is the safest target.</p>
  <p>Past <span class="ex">was</span> can also express real uncertainty rather than a Second Conditional: <span class="ex">If Lena was at the meeting yesterday, she heard the announcement.</span> The speaker does not know whether Lena attended; both clauses refer to a possible real past.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used careful were for hypothetical advice and formal distance, recognized informal was, and separated real past uncertainty from a Second Conditional.">
    <h3>Usage check: follow the stated register and meaning</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Were and was usage choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="were">were</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="was">was</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="informal hypothetical speech">informal hypothetical speech</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="real past uncertainty">real past uncertainty</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Use the conventional careful advice frame: If I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="were" data-hint="If I were you is the safest conventional form for careful advice." data-fix="If I were you, I’d start with a smaller pilot.">choose</button> you, I’d start with a smaller pilot.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Use formal written style for a hypothetical building plan: If Hall A <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="were" data-hint="Careful formal hypothetical style allows were with the singular subject Hall A." data-fix="If Hall A were larger, the center would add a studio.">choose</button> larger, the center would add a studio.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">If I was more organized, I’d volunteer</span>, many U.S. speakers use <span class="ex">was</span> as <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="informal hypothetical speech" data-hint="The sentence imagines a different present, and the register is explicitly informal." data-fix="If I was more organized is a widespread informal hypothetical pattern in U.S. speech.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">If Lena was at the meeting yesterday, she heard the announcement</span> expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="real past uncertainty" data-hint="The speaker is unsure whether a real past attendance event happened." data-fix="The sentence expresses real past uncertainty, not an imagined present or future.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose the result: <span class="ex">would</span>, <span class="ex">could</span>, or <span class="ex">might</span></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>modal result</th><th>speaker’s intended meaning</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">would + base</span></td><td>expected consequence inside the imagined situation</td><td><span class="ex">With more room, we would add a studio.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">could + base</span></td><td>ability or available option inside that situation</td><td><span class="ex">With more room, we could offer recording classes.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">might + base</span></td><td>uncertain possible consequence</td><td><span class="ex">With more room, we might attract new tutors.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">wouldn’t + base</span></td><td>expected negative consequence or absence</td><td><span class="ex">With better soundproofing, neighbors wouldn’t hear the music.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Could</span> may also appear in the condition to imagine ability: <span class="ex">If we could open on Sundays, we would add a weekend class.</span> In every modal result, use the base verb: <span class="ex">would add, could offer, might attract</span>, not <span class="ex">would added</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched expected consequence, available option, uncertain possibility, negative consequence, and imagined ability to the intended modal result.">
    <h3>Result check: choose the exact modal meaning</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Second conditional modal result choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would add">would add</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="could offer">could offer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="might attract">might attract</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wouldn’t hear">wouldn’t hear</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="could open">could open</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The team treats a studio as its expected choice in the imagined larger building: With more room, it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would add" data-hint="Use would for the expected consequence inside the imagined situation." data-fix="With more room, the center would add a recording studio.">choose</button> a recording studio.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The larger room would make recording classes an available option: The center <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="could offer" data-hint="Use could for an ability or option made possible by the imagined room." data-fix="With more room, the center could offer recording classes.">choose</button> recording classes.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The director is unsure whether new tutors would respond: The center <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="might attract" data-hint="Use might for an uncertain possible consequence." data-fix="With more room, the center might attract new tutors.">choose</button> new tutors.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Better soundproofing would prevent a specific problem: Neighbors <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wouldn’t hear" data-hint="Use the negative would form for the expected absence of noise outside." data-fix="With better soundproofing, neighbors wouldn’t hear the music.">choose</button> the music.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The center cannot open Sundays now. Imagine that ability in the condition: If it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="could open" data-hint="Use could in the if-clause to imagine an ability the center does not currently have." data-fix="If the center could open on Sundays, it would add a weekend class.">choose</button> on Sundays, it would add a weekend class.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ask a hypothetical question without scrambling the if-clause</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Put question order in the result clause: <span class="ex">What would you change...?</span> Keep statement order in the condition: <span class="ex">...if you led the project?</span> The same division works with <span class="ex">where, who, how</span>, and yes-or-no questions.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You formed what, where, who, and yes-or-no hypothetical questions while preserving statement order inside each if-clause.">
    <h3>Question check: request one clear kind of information</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Second conditional question choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="What would you change">What would you change</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Where would the team meet">Where would the team meet</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Who would lead the class">Who would lead the class</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Would the center stay open">Would the center stay open</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Ask for one project feature: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="What would you change" data-hint="Use What, then would, the subject you, and the base verb change." data-fix="What would you change if you led the project?">choose</button> if you led the project?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ask for a place under a closed-building scenario: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Where would the team meet" data-hint="Use Where before would and keep the subject team before the base verb meet." data-fix="Where would the team meet if this building closed?">choose</button> if this building closed?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ask for a person under an imagined evening schedule: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Who would lead the class" data-hint="Who requests the person; would lead builds the imagined result." data-fix="Who would lead the class if it started at 8:00 p.m.?">choose</button> if it started at 8:00 p.m.?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Ask a yes-or-no question about an imagined lower-rent result: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Would the center stay open" data-hint="Begin with Would, then the subject center and base verb stay." data-fix="Would the center stay open if rent were lower?">choose</button> if rent were lower?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: weak <span class="ex">would</span>, linked questions, and contrast</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Let message words carry the beats</h3>
      <p><span class="ex">Would</span> often contracts after a subject: <span class="ex">I’d /aɪd/, you’d /jud/, we’d /wid/, they’d /ðeɪd/</span>. In <span class="ex">We’d ADD a STUdio</span>, the result verb and new noun carry stronger beats than the contraction.</p>
      <p>After <span class="ex">’d</span>, a base verb points to <span class="ex">would</span>: <span class="ex">We’d add</span>. A past participle points to <span class="ex">had</span>: <span class="ex">We’d added the chairs before noon</span>. The following verb form and context resolve the contraction.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Link without losing the condition</h3>
      <p>In quick U.S. speech, <span class="ex">What would you do?</span> may sound like /wʌt wədʒə ˈdu/. <span class="ex">If I</span> commonly links as /ɪfaɪ/. These are listening targets; a careful full form remains natural.</p>
      <p>Stress the corrected viewpoint when needed: <span class="ex">If the grant ARRIVES, we’ll RENT the hall</span> for the open plan; <span class="ex">If a larger grant ARRIVED, we’d BUILD a studio</span> for the remote scenario.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read open and remote framing, linked hypothetical question, advice, modal result, and contraction-meaning listening check">
    <h2>Listen for stance before opening the transcript</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your tutor reads each line once naturally and once in short thought groups. Decide how the speaker frames the condition and what the modal result contributes.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard remote framing, a linked hypothetical question, tactful advice, an available option, and the difference between would and had in contracted forms.">
